// Shabari — aranya kanda
// Source: Valmiki, Aranya Kanda, sargas 69-75 (verify) (later tradition)
// Migrated from the old script format. Not yet staged for the Ramlila stage.

>> scene forest style=bharni-earth
>> music dark
>> cast rama:exile at 0.3
>> cast lakshmana:exile at 0.2
>> enter kabandha from right to 0.72 in 1.2
>> shake 5
On the road south a thing with no head and a mouth in its belly closes its arms around them both.
>> pose rama swing1
>> pose lakshmana swing1
>> set kabandha pose=hurt
>> sound thud
They cut themselves free, one arm each. It thanks them. It was a gandharva once, and a curse has just ended.
KABANDHA: Burn this body, and I will tell you what I know. Go to the monkey Sugriva on Mount Rishyamukha. He has lost what you have lost. And on the way, by Lake Pampa, an old woman is waiting for you.
>> pose rama stand
>> pose lakshmana stand
>> scene pampa clear
>> music forest
>> enter shabari from right to 0.3 in 0.1
>> set shabari face=1
Shabari was a girl when her teacher Matanga told her: stay here; Rama will come. She is very old now. She has swept the path every day.
>> enter rama:exile from right to 0.8 in 1.4
>> set rama face=-1
>> enter lakshmana:exile from right to 0.9 in 1.6
>> set lakshmana face=-1
SHABARI: You have come. He said you would. Sit. I have only fruit, but I have chosen it.
>> pose rama sit
>> play berries \{"count":8\}
>> pose shabari give
>> move shabari to 0.66 in 1.4
She brings him the sweet ones in her two hands. He eats them all.
RAMA: I have eaten at kings' tables. I have not been fed like this.
SHABARI: Then I have done what I stayed for. With your leave, I will go to my teacher now.
>> prop fire altar at 0.3 scale=0.8
>> move shabari to 0.3 in 1.6
>> set shabari visible=false
>> sound bell
She walks into her fire as into a doorway, and they see her rise from it, young, and go up.
>> move rama to 0.6 in 1
>> pose rama stand
>> music grief
Pampa in spring. The lotuses are open, the bees are loud, and everything that is beautiful hurts him.
RAMA: She would have loved this lake, Lakshmana.
>> wash
-> END
